# Tag trackballs so compositors apply their trackball settings to them.
#
# Install to /etc/udev/rules.d/71-trackball-tags.rules
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# niri (like Mutter, which it copies here) decides which config block governs a
# pointer by reading udev properties, in src/input/mod.rs:
#
#     is_trackball  = udev property ID_INPUT_TRACKBALL is set
#     is_trackpoint = udev property ID_INPUT_POINTINGSTICK is set
#     is_mouse      = pointer capability AND not touchpad/trackball/trackpoint
#
# systemd's own 60-evdev/60-input-id rules only set ID_INPUT_TRACKBALL for
# devices listed in its hwdb, and most Bluetooth/USB trackballs are not in it --
# they come through as plain ID_INPUT_MOUSE. The practical effect is that
# `trackball { }` in the niri config is silently DEAD for those devices, and the
# trackball instead inherits whatever `mouse { }` says, so it can't be given a
# different scroll direction or acceleration from a regular mouse.
#
# Tagging the device here makes `trackball { }` actually govern it.
#
# CHECK YOUR OWN DEVICE with:
#     ~/.config/scripts/niri-inputs.py -v
# and find the match attributes with:
#     udevadm info -a /dev/input/eventN | grep -E 'ATTRS\{(name|id/vendor|id/product)\}'
#
# After editing:
#     sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#     sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input --action=change
# A Bluetooth device may need to be reconnected, and niri re-reads the tag when
# the device is re-added.

# Nulea BT5.0 wireless trackball (Bluetooth 25a7:faa0).
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{id/vendor}=="25a7", ATTRS{id/product}=="faa0", \
    ENV{ID_INPUT_MOUSE}=="1", ENV{ID_INPUT_TRACKBALL}="1"

# Add further trackballs below, one line each, e.g.:
# Kensington Expert Mouse / SlimBlade (USB 047d:*)
# SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{id/vendor}=="047d", ATTRS{id/product}=="2041", \
#     ENV{ID_INPUT_MOUSE}=="1", ENV{ID_INPUT_TRACKBALL}="1"
